{"author":"Rebar Bot","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"## Phase 1 RCA — CORRECTION on reachability (supersedes the reachability claim above)\n\nMy prior comment claimed site 1 (`executor.py:541` unknown scripted step) is a **REACHABLE** defect that reaches the classifier via `_envelope_error`/`_structured_llm_failure`. **That claim is WRONG and I retract it.** A decisive runtime experiment shows the exception is caught *before* it ever reaches `error_code_for`.\n\n**Experiment (runtime oracle).** I instrumented `rebar._errors.error_code_for` to record every exception it classifies, then ran a valid workflow doc whose one step references an unknown scripted `uses:`:\n```\nrun_workflow({... steps:[{id:s1, uses:\"__definitely_not_a_real_step__\"}]})\n -> returns RunResult(status=\"failed\",\n                      error=\"step 's1' failed: unknown scripted step '...' (not in the step registry)\")\n -> error_code_for invocations during the run: []   # NEVER called\n```\nRoot cause of the non-reachability: `interpreter.py:509-511` wraps `_dispatch` in `except Exception as exc:` and converts any step-dispatch raise into `StepResult(status=\"failed\", error=str(exc))`. **The exception TYPE is discarded; only the message string survives in-band.** So the `WorkflowError` never propagates as an exception, the gate ops' `except LLMError` never sees it, and `error_code_for` is never reached. The plan-review/verify-completion gate workflows are also built-ins with only registered steps, so an unknown `uses:` cannot arise there in practice either.\n\n**Corrected legitimacy assessment.** Both bare sites are therefore **LATENT**, not observable, today:\n- `executor.py:541` (unknown step): caught by the interpreter -> failed `RunResult` string. Never classified.\n- `editor.py:475` (missing bundle): CLI-only; the CLI catch prints the raw message. Never classified.\n\n`error_code_for(WorkflowError(\"unknown scripted step ...\"))` **does** statically return `llm_unavailable` — that is a real classifier-contract gap — but there is **no caller on any live surface who receives that code for either site right now**. The misclassification is a latent property of the pure classifier, not an observed runtime symptom.\n\n**Why I am still fixing site 1 (and why it stays within scope).** `error_code_for` is itself the project's contract surface for classification correctness: dbca's own regression suite asserts `error_code_for(...)` **directly** for the analogous WorkflowError subtypes (e.g. `test_workflow_validation_and_version_errors_are_invalid_input`), no live surface required. By that established standard, `error_code_for` mapping an unknown-scripted-step fault to `llm_unavailable` diverges from the dbca taxonomy (found-but-invalid workflow = caller `invalid_input`; only the execute base stays `llm_unavailable`, AC3). The fix:\n- uses **existing** vocabulary (`invalid_input`, already in `KNOWN_ERROR_CODES`) — no new code, **no design decision**;\n- **does not alter observable behavior** on any surface (the site is latent) — it is a contract-completeness / defense-in-depth correction of the classifier, so a future surface that *does* propagate the type classifies it correctly;\n- mirrors exactly what dbca did for sibling subtypes, tested the same way.\n\nSo it stays within \"restore correct classification\" and does not trip the escalate-on-behavior-change / escalate-on-design-decision guard. **I am flagging the latency prominently** so the operator can course-correct: if you would rather NOT land a latent-only classifier correction, say so and I will stop.\n\nSiblings 551b (editor missing-bundle) and 73d8 (ExpressionError) remain escalated design decisions as before — unchanged by this correction (both also latent / new-vocabulary)."},"env_id":"eea68b7f-6697-488b-ac52-744e2ed8e96f","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1788045825864281255,"uuid":"34e186d0-b35f-4264-9b1f-66bcdc8862b2"}